o  competitors who can fly straight over  russian airspace so if you look at  lufthansa going to beijing now they have  to compete against air china who can go  straight across russia and their flight  will take an hour and a half less  next is fuel costs which have been on a  wild ride by march 8 the price of crude  had climbed some 64 higher than the  start of 2022 around the same time the  price of jet fuel was almost double what  it was a year ago airlines have to  somehow recoup the extra cost typically  fuel is about 20 of their costs are  pretty significant but what you're going  to see and there's an alternative is  airlines actually reducing capacity to  force passengers up the yield curve so  they can't take those passengers at the  rock bottom prices anymore now some  airlines especially european ones tend  to hedge their fuel costs which is  essentially where they agree to buy a  certain quantity of fuel for a certain  price at a certain point in the future  in a bid to reduce volatility but for  airlines that are not hedged this is an  immediate hit to their cash flow uh most  of the u.s airlines in the us are  actually not hedged in america there's  only a few major airlines and domestic  demand is recovering strongly so  analysts tell me airlines should be able  to raise ticket prices and still fill a  plane but in europe it's a different  story there the airline market is less  concentrated and consumers have a wider  range of choice what's more analysts  told me that so far demand in europe  hasn't recovered as strongly as it has  in the u.s partly due to a patchwork of  covert regulations putting off  travellers what this means is that if  carriers that were unhedged when fuel  prices shot up like hungary's wizair  tried to raise ticket prices to  recapture some of those rising fuel  costs analysts told me they may struggle  to fill a plane in a statement wizair  said the company had since capped its  fuel cost exposure for the next four  months  so let's go back to this chart  does it mean that airlines are in for  yet another turbulent year well  according to one analyst for those that  aren't exposed to the crisis in ukraine  things might be okay yes i'm so  confident the industry will recover in  2022 because we've come off such a low  base some carriers earnings gonna be  fairly good this year despite what's  going on but those that are exposed now  face a difficult period on the back of a  difficult two years there are certain  routes and maybe airlines with weaker  balance sheets weaker business models  that now will fail it's because while  governments came to the rescue of many  airlines throughout the corona virus  pandemic analysts tell me that high fuel  prices or closed airspace is unlikely to  trigger a similar government aid  response that means that for some  airlines after two years of enduring the  pandemic even the slightest detour on  the route to recovery might be the final  straw  hi there this channel focuses on  transport and mobility so if you're  interested in that feel free to  subscribe however if you want to know  more about the conflict in ukraine then  my colleagues have been doing a great  job of covering the latest from that  region i've left the link below please  go check it out  [Music]  you
 

